265
00:31:41.510 --> 00:31:45.789
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): He misbehaved a bit. You can see my screen, okay.

266
00:31:46.710 --> 00:31:56.850
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): Yep, okay, so… A couple of weeks ago, we… as part of the VTA, Introduced the concept of…

267
00:31:57.170 --> 00:32:06.759
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): how do you delegate control of what's in the VTA to third parties without giving up custodial ownership of this? And so…

268
00:32:06.960 --> 00:32:10.479
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): really, what I'm gonna do is just run through

269
00:32:10.870 --> 00:32:18.070
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): How this works for a real-world use case that is extremely common, and every single one of us

270
00:32:18.200 --> 00:32:26.729
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): that uses any form of the Decentralized trust Graph is going to have to go through at some point. So, this is…

271
00:32:27.380 --> 00:32:41.210
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): very common, very real. The user experience is a bit rough around the edges, so it's more of a demonstration of it, but I think you'll see there's lots of opportunities where improvements can be made.

272
00:32:41.570 --> 00:32:48.320
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): So, really, if you step back from it, most identity systems or authorization systems.

273
00:32:48.510 --> 00:33:02.569
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): they're really actually are trying to figure out who has the authority to authorize something, and in most identity systems, it's an all-or-nothing approach. You kind of either have it or you don't. In VTI,

274
00:33:02.680 --> 00:33:17.140
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): that is all linked to two assets. One is the decentralized identifier, the DID that represents your identity and the keys, and then your set of credentials that get presented along with the DID.

275
00:33:17.530 --> 00:33:20.139
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): So that you basically have

276
00:33:20.410 --> 00:33:29.540
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): both the identity and the claims that you are proving ownership of as part of this. We…

277
00:33:29.840 --> 00:33:39.109
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): as the reference point for VTI, WebVH is the DID method that we use as kind of the… the first-class citizen.

278
00:33:39.250 --> 00:33:52.350
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): In that scenario. And WebVH has some interesting characteristics about it, which is every time you modify it, you have to sign that chain. It's kind of like a blockchain,

279
00:33:52.410 --> 00:34:12.259
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): self-certifying identity chain as it goes through this. So, to edit it, I have to have access to the secret keys that allow me to update it. Those keys are actually hashed in advance, so that you can predict what the next log entry would look like. Now, if you lose control of those keys.

280
00:34:12.260 --> 00:34:18.970
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): anybody can take over your DID. So, very high security aspect.

281
00:34:18.980 --> 00:34:30.080
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): And so, if I want to edit my DID document, I want to change my name, I want to put an agent name in, I want to change a service endpoint, I want to rotate my keys.

282
00:34:30.330 --> 00:34:40.979
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): I have to re-sign all of this information. So, I kind of have 3 options. Option 1 is I could put all my secret keys into the browser.

283
00:34:41.239 --> 00:34:45.870
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): not a great idea. Browsers are inherently…

284
00:34:46.190 --> 00:34:55.380
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): Bad, insecure, no sense of privacy, and trying to keep boundaries and partitioning within your browser

285
00:34:55.580 --> 00:35:02.960
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): is a bit of a, science or art form to it as you go through it, so you don't really want to rely on the browser.

286
00:35:03.080 --> 00:35:17.990
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): The second most common option in the industry today is that you effectively give your keys to the third party. You're giving up custodial ownership and management of this. Actually, WhatsApp, Signal, all of those, you're kind of giving…

287
00:35:18.000 --> 00:35:26.940
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): Well, you don't really give your keys. They're giving keys, but they're actually maintaining control of that. Apple, Google, they all work this way as well.

288
00:35:27.020 --> 00:35:44.480
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): The problem with that is you have to have implicit trust in whoever is your third-party manager for this, and if they choose to be a bad actor, there's really nothing you can do about it. They've got your keys, they can kind of rewrite your identity as you go through this.

289
00:35:44.540 --> 00:35:47.570
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): So, the third option, then, would be

290
00:35:47.790 --> 00:35:50.540
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): Let's not give the keys to anyone.

291
00:35:50.980 --> 00:36:05.609
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): let's move the authorization flow instead. And this is where trust tasks and signing and some advanced, kind of, cryptography that's built around the DIDs and VC concept start to come into play.

292
00:36:06.270 --> 00:36:10.670
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): And to help this, we kind of had 5 design rules.

293
00:36:10.790 --> 00:36:24.999
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): The first one is that the VTA is the only trusted component. It holds the keys, it decides, it enforces. It's kind of judge-jury execution for yourself all in one.

294
00:36:25.030 --> 00:36:44.909
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): I say that for where it is today. It's likely in the future, some of that functionality may be split out into a mesh of VTAs, or even into the VTCs, in form of, kind of, witnessing and other aspects. The second design rule is that third-party websites are untrusted.

295
00:36:45.230 --> 00:36:48.250
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): They're not in your control.

296
00:36:49.130 --> 00:37:00.229
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): they can propose changes that they would like to make to your data that's within the VTA, but it can't authorize it, and it can't…

297
00:37:00.340 --> 00:37:09.689
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): write a word that I'm reading when deciding. So what actually happens is when I make a change on that third-party website, and you'll see this in the demo.

298
00:37:09.730 --> 00:37:26.030
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): That is packaged up as a trust cast. The VTA is actually analyzing that, and then constructing a new message from what it believes the differential changes that you're making, and presents that back to the human, which the human then is verifying, and there's,

299
00:37:26.030 --> 00:37:37.839
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): you know, kind of a linkage or a fingerprint that goes along with this as we go through this. The third rule was that devices are semi-trusted and revocable. So whether that be

300
00:37:38.010 --> 00:37:46.319
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): A browser with a plugin, or your mobile phone asking with a pop-up, a push notification, saying that

301
00:37:46.570 --> 00:37:48.820
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): They can be compromised.

302
00:37:48.980 --> 00:37:57.450
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): you need to be able to revoke the device, and you may actually want more than one device to approve things. And so.

303
00:37:57.560 --> 00:38:13.340
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): The VTA is extremely programmable. You can have policies, you can say, I want all of my devices to approve it. I may even want a family member or a close friend to approve things for certain tasks as I go through this.

304
00:38:13.340 --> 00:38:18.729
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): Fourth is that the approval is decoupled from the request. So…

305
00:38:19.000 --> 00:38:24.289
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): Whoever is the requester of that change, they cannot be the approver.

306
00:38:24.730 --> 00:38:32.200
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): And so what we're doing is having separation of duty within the system. And so, for example, if I make the…

307
00:38:32.200 --> 00:38:48.390
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): change in my browser, my phone, second device, must be the one that approves it. If I make it on my phone, then something else on the phone with a different identity or another device has to be the one that approves that as you go through it. So again, just trying to…

308
00:38:49.130 --> 00:39:01.830
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): minimize the risk of an attacker grabbing your unlocked laptop at a cafe and changing everything and locking you out of your systems. And then, the fifth and last one is

309
00:39:02.670 --> 00:39:10.499
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): When you approve something, that approval is actually a signed crypto… cryptographic,

310
00:39:10.800 --> 00:39:25.940
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): payload that can't be tampered with. And that goes back to the VTA, and the VTA then knows what was proposed by the website, what did it think was the difference, what has been approved by the human through one or more

311
00:39:26.140 --> 00:39:37.060
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): approval steps, and then cryptographically applies that all back together in and says, if that all matches, then I will make the change, as you go through it. So…

312
00:39:37.200 --> 00:39:49.700
Glenn Gore (Affinidi Pte Ltd): you know, kind of really simply, nothing changes state unless the agent has verified, kind of single-use device signed approval matching the exact action. And it's checked again at the moment of execution.

313
00:39:49.770 --> 00:40:04.460
